docs {
  # Namespaces Registry Utility Service Tests
  
  Tests for the SIF 3.7 Namespaces Registry Utility Service endpoints.
  
  ## What Are Namespaces?
  
  Namespaces are a centralized registry of XML namespace URIs and their
  corresponding schema location URLs that are currently valid within the
  Environment. Consumers and providers use the registry to stay synchronized
  and prevent namespace mismatches while exchanging messages. Namespaces
  support zone-scoping: global entries can be overridden by zone-specific
  entries with the same URI.
  
  ## URL Pattern
  
  Namespace endpoints are scoped to a domain and solution:
  - `POST /{domainId}/{solutionId}/namespaces/namespace` — Create a namespace (admin only)
  - `GET /{domainId}/{solutionId}/namespaces` — List namespaces (zone-scoped)
  - `GET /{domainId}/{solutionId}/namespaces/{id}` — Get namespace by ID
  - `PUT /{domainId}/{solutionId}/namespaces/{id}` — Update namespace (admin only)
  - `DELETE /{domainId}/{solutionId}/namespaces/{id}` — Delete namespace (admin only)
  
  ## Tests Included
  
  - Create namespace (admin, valid payload — broker assigns UUID id)
  - List namespaces (verify collection format)
  - Get namespace by ID
  - Update namespace (full replacement)
  - Delete namespace
  - Create namespace — non-admin rejected (403)
  - Zone scoping query — global + zone-specific override by URI
  
  ## Prerequisites
  
  - A domain and solution must exist (createdDomainId, createdSolutionId)
  - A namespaces provider must be registered in the environment-global zone
  - Valid authentication credentials
  
  ## Important Notes
  
  - Namespaces support full CRUD (unlike alerts which are immutable)
  - Only admin environments can create, update, or delete namespaces
  - Any authenticated environment can query namespaces
  - PUT does full replacement of the entire namespace object
  - Zone-scoping: zone-specific entries override global entries with the same uri
  - The `id` is a broker-assigned UUID (not user-provided like code sets)
  - Namespaces are flat objects: zone, uri, url (no nested child objects)
}
